request cancellation (notifications/cancelled) -- implementation plan
=======================================================================

status: done (2026-08-20)
spec: "request cancellation" (D-2026-08-20-request-cancellation), depends
on the fork-per-request dispatch loop from spec "the serve loop is
concurrent" (D-2026-08-20-concurrent-serve-loop; no separate plans/ file
exists for that change). test:
mcp/test/repro_request_cancel.py.

the protocol surface
---------------------

client sends, at any time after a request it issued is still believed
in-progress:

    {"jsonrpc": "2.0", "method": "notifications/cancelled",
     "params": {"requestId": <the request's own id>, "reason": "..."}}

a notification (no id of its own, no reply possible or expected).
server behavior per the mcp spec (2025-06-18, "basic/utilities/
cancellation"):
- MUST NOT send a response for the cancelled request
- MAY ignore it if the id is unknown, already completed, or the request
  cannot be cancelled -- never an error
- the client MUST NOT cancel its own `initialize`; nothing enforces this
  server-side (a client-side obligation, not ours to police)

why two halves, not one
-------------------------

a tools/call that resolves to an ML tool goes through
MCP_Backend.ml_run -> MCP.run_tool: the scala thread sends the protocol
command and BLOCKS (Promise.join) while the ML side runs the tool on its
own Future.forks task (interrupts = true, since 2026-07-28). the scala
thread and the ml fork are two SEPARATE things that happen to be
connected by a promise -- interrupting the scala thread only ever
touches the scala side of that connection.

confirmed by direct measurement (mcp/test/repro_request_cancel.py, a
tool that sleeps 6s then writes a completion marker file), cancelling
1s in:
- scala half only (ML side's cancel_run call disabled for the
  measurement): client gets no response, its connection frees up in
  ~0.1s -- but the marker still appears at t=6.06s. the ml fork ran the
  full sleep regardless of the cancel.
- both halves wired up (shipped): the marker never appears. the prover's
  own sleep is genuinely interrupted, not merely abandoned.

design
------

SCALA HALF (mcp/src/mcp_server.scala, serve()):
- `cancellable: Synchronized[Map[JSON.T, Cancel_State]]`, keyed by the
  json-rpc request id. `Cancel_State` is `Active(thread: Isabelle_Thread)`
  once the forked thread has registered itself, or `Cancel_Pending` if a
  cancel notification named this id before that registration happened.
- dispatch() intercepts `notifications/cancelled` BEFORE forking: parses
  `params.requestId`, looks it up. Active -> ask the ML side to cancel
  (handler.cancel(id), see below) THEN interrupt the thread (in that
  order -- see races below). Cancel_Pending already there -> no-op.
  Absent -> insert Cancel_Pending (covers both "arrived early" and
  "unknown/already completed": indistinguishable at this lookup, see
  tradeoff below).
- an ordinary dispatched request registers itself ATOMICALLY as the
  very first thing its forked thread does: check-or-set against the
  same map. finding Cancel_Pending there means skip the handler
  entirely (nobody wants the result); otherwise proceed and register
  Active(self). Removed (whichever the outcome) in the thread's own
  finally, alongside the existing in_flight decrement -- unchanged
  accounting, so the shutdown drain still works.
- a genuine interrupt propagating out of handler.handle_line is caught
  and swallowed quietly (no reply, no error log) -- distinguished from
  every other exception, which still gets logged as a request failure
  as before.

ML HALF (mcp/src/mcp_session.scala + mcp/Tools/MCP_Tools.thy):
- `MCP_Backend.ml_run` grows an `on_dispatch: String => Unit = _ => ()`
  callback, called with the internal MCP.run_tool id the moment it is
  minted, before the call blocks. Default no-op so every existing
  caller/backend (Fake_Backend, tests) is unaffected without touching
  their own signatures beyond adding (and ignoring) the parameter.
- `Handler` (mcp_server.scala) holds `cancel_targets: Synchronized[Map[
  JSON.T, String]]` (json-rpc id -> internal ml_run id), set by
  on_dispatch around the ml_run call in the tools/call case, removed in
  a finally once ml_run returns by any means. `handler.cancel(id)` reads
  it and calls `backend.cancel_run(internal_id)` if present.
- `MCP_Backend.cancel_run(id: String): Unit = ()` (default no-op).
  `MCP_Session.cancel_run` sends the new protocol command
  `MCP.cancel_tool` with that id.
- ML side (MCP_Tools.thy): `run_tool_cancellers: (unit -> unit)
  Symtab.table Synchronized.var`, entered right after MCP.run_tool forks
  its worker (`fn () => Future.cancel result`), removed from BOTH ends
  whichever fires first -- MCP.cancel_tool itself, and the existing
  result-posting fork (Symtab.delete_safe makes the second removal
  harmless either way). `MCP.cancel_tool [id]` looks the id up and calls
  the thunk if present, else no-op. mirrors Scala.function's OWN
  cancel_scala (Pure/System/scala.ML, Pure/System/scala.scala): the side
  giving up on a wait tells the side doing the work to stop, by id,
  rather than relying on an interrupt reaching across the bridge on its
  own -- same pattern, opposite direction (there: ML waits on scala;
  here: scala waits on ML).
- the existing two-future shape in MCP.run_tool (worker fork + a
  dependent, non-interruptible posting fork) is exactly what keeps this
  safe: Future.cancel assigns the worker's future an interrupt result
  (it does not leave it unfinished), so the posting fork's
  Future.join_result returns immediately with `Exn.Exn`, maps to
  `("error", ...)`, and still posts MCP.run_tool_result -- the comment
  already on that code ("if the worker were interrupted, posting from
  within it would never run") describes exactly the hang this avoids;
  cancellation does not introduce a new instance of it, confirmed by
  reading Future.cancel/cancel_group/assign_result in Pure/Concurrent/
  future.ML.

races (all covered by repro_request_cancel.py)
------------------------------------------------

- cancel before the request's thread has registered: Cancel_Pending,
  above.
- cancel after completion, or for an id never seen: same lookup miss as
  "before registration" -- both become a Cancel_Pending insert, which is
  a no-op from the caller's point of view. ACCEPTED TRADEOFF: this holds
  one map entry per such cancel for the rest of the connection, rather
  than tracking every id ever dispatched just to tell "early" apart from
  "unknown". bounded by legitimate traffic (ids are not expected to
  repeat within one connection), not by an attacker-controlled input
  independent of request volume.
- two cancels for one id: second is either a redundant (harmless)
  interrupt of an already-interrupted thread, or lands on an id already
  removed and becomes the Cancel_Pending no-op above.
- notifications/cancelled itself is handled SYNCHRONOUSLY on the reader
  thread, never forked -- forking it would let it race its own target's
  registration in either direction; staying on the single sequential
  reader is what makes the "before registration" case analyzable at
  all. it does not increment in_flight -- nothing to drain, it is fully
  processed by the time dispatch() returns.
- ML-side cancel is requested BEFORE the scala thread is interrupted
  (cancel() in serve(), calls handler.cancel(id) then thread.interrupt()):
  cancel_targets is only removed by the ml_run call's OWN thread-local
  finally once ml_run returns, so asking first -- while the target
  thread is certainly still blocked on the prover -- avoids racing that
  removal.

testing
-------

[scala]: signature-only changes to MCP_Backend/MCP_Session/Fake_Backend/
Throwing_Backend (on_dispatch, cancel_run) -- no new unit tests needed
beyond the existing bridge suite continuing green (`bridge: ml_run round
trip` etc., mcp_test/src/mcp_bridge_tests.scala), since none of those
exercise cancellation itself.

[e2e]: mcp/test/repro_request_cancel.py, modeled on
repro_concurrent_serve.py -- registers a slow (`napper`) ML tool via
repl_step that sleeps then marks completion, fires tools/call without
waiting, cancels ~1s in, and checks: no response arrives for the
cancelled id; a request sent right after gets its reply promptly (the
connection is not stuck behind the cancelled one); the completion
marker's timing (the prover-side measurement above); cancel for an
unknown id and a duplicate cancel for an already-finished id are both
silent no-ops that leave the connection healthy.

regressions checked green: mcp/test/test_mcp.py (103 PASS, exit 0),
mcp/test/repro_concurrent_serve.py, `isabelle mcp_test -b`.

found live during implementation (not this change's bug, not fixed
here): `serve replies per line, skips blanks, stops the backend on EOF`
(mcp_test/src/mcp_handler_tests.scala) flakes under heavy concurrent
load (roughly 1 in 2-3 `mcp_test -b` runs) -- reproduced identically
against a clean build of the code exactly as it stood before this plan
(commit 85c6afd, no cancellation changes at all), so it predates this
work and is not chased here, same policy as the pre-existing `T1:
Isar_Ref toc` 30s-timeout flake already documented.

open questions / not done
---------------------------

- no TTL or cap on the Cancel_Pending bookkeeping (see races above) --
  not asked for, and the bound in practice is legitimate request
  volume, not an independent attacker knob.
- cancelling a request that is still in the SCALA-only portion of
  tools/call (name resolution, scope read, before ml_run is reached) is
  covered by the scala half (thread interrupt) but has nothing for
  cancel_run to reach -- correct and intentional: there is no ML-side
  work yet to cancel at that point.
